S\nSupport resources\nFree & low cost student resources\nResources\nReligious observances\nCareer & professional development\nCareer planning\nProfessional development\nPath of Professorship\nTeaching at MIT\nWriting\nStudent leadership\nConference Grant\nEntrepreneurship\nStudent looking to start a company\nStudent who already formed a company\nServing on the board of an MIT member\u2019s existing company\nReport bias\nGraduate Policies and Procedures\nStudent finances & employment\nGraduate appointments\nFinancial assistance and grants\nGuaranteed Transitional Support\nTransition Support Coordinators\nMIT Grant for Graduate Students with Children (GGSC)\nGraduate Student Short-Term Emergency Fund\nDoctoral Long-Term Financial Hardship Funding\nFood resources\nFinding employment\nFellowships\nCareer & professional development\nLoans\nFinancial wellbeing\nBudgeting\nCredit\nIdentity theft\nWorkshops\nTaxes\nSaving\nInvesting\nTransitioning out of school\nHelpful links\nSupport for activities and community\nTuition and fees\nGraduate student benefits\nGraduate Admissions\nAbout MIT\nDegree programs\nFields of study\nDoctoral degrees\nMaster\u2019s degrees\nCertificate & online programs\nExchange, special, & visiting students\nAdmissions process\nProcedures\nStandardized tests\nGraduate Record Examination (GRE)\nInternational English Language Testing System (IELTS)\nTest of English as a Foreign Language (TOEFL iBT)\nDuolingo English Test (DET)\nCambridge English Qualifications\nInternational applicants\nApplication fee waivers\nGraduate admissions policies\nGraduate Admissions privacy statement\nEnglish Language Proficiency test requirement\nEnglish Language Proficiency test exemptions\nDepartment English Language Proficiency test waivers\nCosts & funding\nCost of attendance\nSalaries and stipends\nLiving expenses\nAdditional considerations and information\nProspective student resources\nGraduate application resources for prospective students\nAdmitted student resources\nSupporting you at MIT: Health & wellbeing\nThe \u201cwhole you\u201d at MIT\nHousing: Expanded availability and new options for on-campus housing\nGradAdmissions Help Desk\nCommunity & belonging\nCommunities & networks\nInternational students\nWomen\nLGBTQ+\nCultural student organizations\nFirst-generation and/or low-income (FLI)\nStudents with disabilities\nVeterans\nCommunity resources\nGradThriving\nOGE Grad Families\nOGE GradDiversity\nSign up for the newsletter\nInitiatives for prospective students\nGradCatalyst\nMIT Mondays\nGraduate Application Assistance Programs (GAAP)\nMIT Summer Research Program\nRecruitment events\nPrograms for current students\nMIT Mondays\nGraduate Student Appreciation Week\nFunds for grad activities\nRequired training\nAwards & recognition\nCommitted to Caring (C2C)\nC2C honoree index\nC2C Selection Committee\nNominate faculty\nMentoring guideposts\nThe Goodwin Medal\nPrevious Recipients\nEnduring Impact Honor\n2026 Enduring Impact honorees\nGet involved\nGraduate Community Fellows\nDonate to graduate community\nGraduate Student Orientation\nOrientation Slack community guidelines\nOGE social media\nGRE Instructions\nDepending on the program that interests you, official GRE scores may or may not be required for admission. Each academic department determines its own policy regarding the GRE General Test and GRE Subject Test. Please check the Degree Programs directory for more information about the program\u2019s specific requirements for the GRE exam.\nThe entrance fee for the GRE General Test costs $220 in most countries. Further information about GRE fees can be found online. To register for the GRE, please visit www.ets.org/gre. When submitting your test scores to MIT, please use Institution Code 3514; codes for the Sloan School of Management may vary.\nScore Reporting\nMIT GradAdmissions advises that applicants plan to take any standardized tests as early as possible as verification processing time varies widely. All self-reported scores will need to be verified before the department deadline set by your program of interest.\nSend your GRE scores to MIT using institutional code 3514. Be advised: codes for the MIT Sloan School of Management may vary.\nMinimum score requirement\nThere is no minimum score requirement for the GRE; scores are reviewed as a part of a holistic evaluation process.\nCheck for the specific requirements of the graduate program that interests you by navigating to their information page from the Degree Programs directory.\nGRE Home Edition\nMIT GradAdmissions encourages applicants to take the GRE at a testing center if possible. If you are unable to take the exam at a testing center due to extenuating circumstances (for example, if there are no open testing centers in your region), you may take the GRE Home Edition.\nGeneral test policies\nDepartments determine their own GRE requirements. More instructions can be found directly in the application on the Test Scores Instructions page. The general policy information can be found below.\nGRE Required\nApplicants applying to departments that require GRE scores will not be able to submit their application(s) until scores are reported on the Test Scores page of the application.\nGradAdmissions only requires applicants to submit self-reported (unofficial) scores at the time of submission, but all GRE test scores must be verified by the test provider in order for your application to be reviewed by your department of interest.\nBe advised, any test scores submitted on one application will be visible on any other applications of programs that require GRE scores or give you the option to list GRE scores.\nGRE Optional\nIf your program of interest lists the GRE as optional, you are not required to submit GRE test scores in order to submit your application, but you may do so if you believe your scores bolster your application.\nBe advised, any scores listed will be considered application materials for review by committee.\nGRE not accepted\nIf your program of interest does not accept GRE scores, they will not consider GRE scores in the evaluation of your application. If you have shared your scores in other applications, these scores will not be visible.\nMIT Office of Graduate Education\n77 Massachusetts Avenue\nRoom 3-107\nCambridge, MA 02139-4307\nContact Us:\ngrad-ed@mit.edu\n(617) 253-4860\nMIT Privacy Policy\nAccessibility\nGradLink Social Media:\nGradDiversity Social Media:\ngue@mit.edu\n\u00d7\nSearch\nThis site uses cookies to give you the best possible experience. By browsing our website, you agree to our use of cookies.\nIf you require further information, please visit the Privacy Policy page.\nI accept", "links": [["", "https://oge.mit.edu/"], ["Search", "https://oge.mit.edu/graduate-admissions/applications/standardized-tests/graduate-record-examination-gre/"], ["Blog & Events", "https://oge.mit.edu/news-and-events/"], ["Contact Us", "https://oge.mit.edu/services-team/contact-us/"], ["Services & Team", "https://oge.mit.edu/services-team/"], ["", "https://oge.mit.edu/graduate-admissions/applications/standardized-tests/graduate-record-examination-gre/"], ["Welcome", "https://oge.mit.edu/services-team/welcome-from-the-director/"], ["About us", "https://oge.mit.edu/services-team/about-us/"], ["Staff", "https://oge.mit.edu/services-team/staff/"], ["Services", "https://oge.mit.edu/services-team/services/"], ["Contact us", "https://oge.mit.edu/services-team/contact-us/"], ["Student Support & Development", "https://oge.mit.edu/student-support-development/"], ["", "https://oge.mit.edu/graduate-admissions/applications/standardized-tests/graduate-record-examination-gre/"], ["Student support & development", "https://oge.mit.edu/student-support-development/"], ["GradSupport", "https://oge.mit.edu/student-support-development/gradsupport/"], ["REFS", "https://oge.mit.edu/student-support-development/refs/"], ["Support resources", "https://oge.mit.edu/student-support-development/support-resources/"